"guns" <sungmustpae@gmail.com> writes:

> In learning to write patches for Nethack, I've noticed that everyone
> includes the date and time in the headers. This seems very official

The diff utility puts it there, there's nothing more to it, nothing as
"official" as you may think.

> and rigorous, but I've been feeling lazy and wondering what exactly
> their utility is. Doesn't the OS automatically show the date of last
> modification anyway?

Yeah, that's exactly what the diff utility shows there. The timestamps of
the files on the disk where the diffs are extracted, the original and
modified versions.

> The "patch" man page implies that their inclusion is for the benefit
> of the --set-utc option, but what is the benefit of that? Is it for
> keeping binary distros tidy?

Basically it is for informational value, you can see what date the system
clock showed when the modifications were saved. I think the information is
most useful for the person who made the diffs, so they can check that the
diff was extracted between the right versions or something like that.

